they are still  capable of being transferred from one  bacterium to another or whether they are  essentially dormant and will not be  mobile and so the researchers came up  with a very nice inverse PCR based  targeted  approach just getting at those elements  sequencing them and then because of the  long read lengths of Peck bio you get  the flanking sequences of those  antibiotic resistance genes that allow  you to determine whether or not they're  within mobile genetic elements or not so  this is going at a specific question  that's at the heart of many studies  looking at the resistance the antibiotic  resistance and being very efficient in  pulling out the information that you  want the last example also in the area  of antibiotic resistance genes was  looking at a cow manure and playing  smart sequencing to significantly extend  the roster of functional antibiotic  resistance genes found in animal gut  bacteria the problem here is that  because we've treated livestock with  antibiotics for so many decades that  then goes into the soil and selects for  antibiotic resistant bacteria and so  this study used Fosnight sequencing  actually to get a much better  understanding and have lots of  discoveries of new antibiotic resistance  genes on the left on this bar chart only  the leftmost bar is something that has  any match in the GenBank database but  blast all the other bars in the middle  are new discoveries and have essentially  no similarity with GenBank and then on  the right in this picture you see that  again because of the long read links you  get an immediate understanding not just  of the antibiotic resistance gene but  also how they are organized within their  hosts within those bacteria and what  particular genes are right next with  cetera so there are many other  publications that I can go into here's a  list and I invite you to look at our  website under PEC be comm publications  we have a complete list and searchable  database of all the publications so then  lastly on the topic of transcriptomes  and epigenomes we're excited about  collaboration with New England Biolabs  looking at full-length microbial RNA  seek so as you know the read links have  been utilized in I so seek in eukaryotes  for quite a few years and we have  adapted  protocol with the smart capital sikri  agents from any be to make that possible  also now in bacteria and in metagenomic  communities and of course that's  exciting because you don't only want to  know what's there but in many cases you  want actively growing what is being  expressed and how it's being regulated  and so this paper highlights the  methodology and first applications that  you now get a much more complete and  comprehensive view of bacterial  transcriptomes you can see here the  operands being expressed and each line  each of those gray lines represents a  full-length transcript and you can see  already visually very clearly that it's  not nearly as simple as having just  transcripts that span the operon in many  cases one or two genes either in the  beginning or the end are missing so  there's a lot of regulation that is much  more difficult to get at with shorter e  technologies and is immediately apparent  with the long repack bio sequencing and  then finally with regard to the DNA  modification detection this is to  characterize the chemical modifications  that are put on DNA by the bacterial  methylation marks and six methyl adenine  four methyl cytosine 5 methylcytosine we  described I think about six or seven  years ago that the progression of the  polymerase is delayed by the presence of  a methyl group in the template strand so  the polymerase runs along the DNA and  then if it encounters a methylated base  that methylated base acts as a little  speed bump and the polymerase will take  a little bit longer time to get past  that and we measure that in real-time  and thereby can infer whether or not  they wear methylation marks on the DNA  this has been described it published  upon in many papers well over 150 papers  again I've listed them here but they're  all available in our publication  database which is the link is on the  bottom and so the community has  recognized that with PacBio sequencing  we have now entered a new era of  bacterial epi  and this was an older review article and  top a newer one from a few months ago  looking at so-called phase variance  those are variation in bacteria with  regard to the pathogenicity often that  are mediated by methylation and this is  an excellent review article summarizing  the work that has been done in this  particular area there all this  information is being recorded and  curated by the rebase database that's  curated by New England Biolabs and you  can see there well over 5500 records by  now the PEC bio data and if you have  bacterial pack bio data please submit  them to the rebase database so that the  entire community can benefit from that  information even if you don't use it for  your own research it's very important  biological information that is useful  for others with regard to methylation in  metagenomics there are a number of  examples highlighting that this is an  important topic this is a paper from  Eric Triplett in Florida looking at the  methylation of gut microbiomes  and asking the question whether in  conjunction with a disease in this  particular case type 1 diabetes and the  development of autoimmunity against this  disease whether there are differences  and the paper describes the profound  differences in the dominant bacterium  that was a Bacteroides in the extent of  damn methylation between two children  that had a high genetic risk for type 1  diabetes and a year prior to the  development of type 1 diabetes in one of  these children but not in the other so  this is certainly no proof but it's an  indication that we should look at the  methylation status of the gut microbiome  in addition to just the sequences this  is an area from the agriculture and  example from the agricultural space you  know 12 years ago there was a paper  showing that there are dramatic  differences in the methylation changes  during symbiosis of these bacteria that  form associations with the root nodules  of nitrogen fixing bacteria that form in  symbiosis with  and of course back then there was no  commercial smart sequencing and so this  was a paper just looking at the  transition from the free living state to  the nodule of those bacteria in the soil  and finding that there are dramatic  methylation changes so I'd always hope  that someone would apply smart  sequencing and this was then done 10  years later in 2016  again by Eric Triplett looking in detail  about the methylation and gene  expression data in the development of  one of those bacteria during symbiosis  and their dramatic changes 35% of all  the genes are affected there are over  ten percent of these methylation changes  are in this what he called symbiosis  Islands you can see that on the bottom  that is denoted by the two dashed lines  there's a region in the genome that  changes dramatically both with regard to  methylation and expression and the  methylation is there's one methylation  motif that's specific to symbiosis and  768 genes have methylation and  significant expression changes so this  isn't a phenomenon that you absolutely  have to measure and understand if you  want to understand symbiosis and the  interaction of bacteria with plants in  this particular case and then finally  one quite recent example from Gong Fong  at Mount Sinai School of Medicine in New  York City looking at harnessing and  leveraging the methylation information  to improve binning for these metagenomic  communities and this Nature  Biotechnology paper he looked at the  endogenous epigenetic barcodes the  methylation signals that he can then use  as barcodes from the PacBio data to  resolve either the individual reads or  the assembled contacts into species and  strain level pins it allowed him to link  the plasmids and other mobile genetic  elements to their host species because  they carry the same methylation marks  they're in the same bacterial cell so  the methyl transferases will methylate  both the chromosome and the plasmids and  mobile genetic elements and so it very  nicely complements existing methods to  enable more accurate sequence pinning  and so if you're interested in that I  encourage you to look at bounce paper so  with that I'll close and just summarize  and I mentioned that you know I talked  about sequencing performance but of  course cost and the workflow is also  very important for making it into  efficient assays and making that routine  and so with regard to the workflow and  the cost we have introduced an  accelerated library prep or Express  library prep as we call it previously  the library prep took about a day but  with this new protocol that we rolled  out a few months ago it's significantly  faster it only takes three hours and  requires less starting material about  two and a half times less it's more  efficient because you handle the DNA  differently it's just an ad only  workflow and because you're handling the  DNA less there's also less damage to the  DNA and so you have longer read links  and improved sequencing performance and  of course it's amenable to automation  and this is currently available for the  long fragment protocols with regard to  the cost on one example is we forgot to  barcodes  just like any other technologies pick  bio sequencing has barcodes 384 of them  that have been optimized for smart  sequencing which then allows you to  multiplex hundreds of targets and  samples pull them together and sequence  them on a single smart cell the on the  bottom then the analysis software  automatically demultiplexers and puts  each barcode in a separate bin and then  does the analysis CCS or multi molecule  consensus and this can be scaled up  quite dramatically here's a paper  recently published entitled the sequel  to Sanger so it's equal to Sanger  sequencing Amla Khan sequencing that  scales by Paul Hebert and colleagues  from the biodiversity Institute in  Guelph Canada and they bar-coded and  pooled up to 10,000 amplicons in a  single smart cell and found that it was  both more accurate  and in in that the smart sequencing  provided more complete coverage  especially for amplicons before more  polymer tracks which are more  challenging for Sanger sequencing and  also more cost-effective 40-fold cheaper  compared to Sanger sequencing and still  an order of magnitude less expensive  compared to both alumina and iron  torrent so I realized that there's a  strong notion in the community that it's  about cost per base and so forth but we  like to think about it more in terms of  cost per project because sequencing  applications are now very different and  so different technologies have different  strengths and weaknesses and so a  technology that may be too expensive in  one area may actually be much cheaper in  another and so you really can't sort of  globally say cost per base because that  would not be appropriate over all the  different application spaces so as a  case in point Paul and colleagues ran a  project with Sanger sequencing here no  characterizing 200th s immense that cost  them 1.2 million dollars or about 120  million 120 thousand dollars with  chaudhary technologies and now it's you  know only thirty six thousand dollars so  about tenfold less than with aluminum  iron torrent and dramatically less with  with Sanger so with that I would like to  close and I hope that I've given you a  good overview of the unique capabilities  that picked bio sequencing can provide  in the area of metagenomics in the area  of targeted sequencing 16s or you can  adapt that to other genes of interest  that you're interested in we forgot to  shotgun metagenomics being able to get  much more contiguous information on two  or three kilobase fragments and getting  complete microbial genomes from these  representatives of these communities and  then emerging applications harnessing  the Peck biotechnology both for our new  research for transcriptomes  and very important the methylation  information for the epigenomes and so  with that I'll stop this is my  knowledge meant slide actually because  you notice that most of the things that  I talked about we're not done by us but  by the scientific community and so we  are extremely grateful for their efforts  and time applying the Peck biotechnology  in many cases moving it forward and we  are indebted to the researchers efforts  and it's been a great journey and  collaborations with all of them to apply  Peck biome to metagenomics but also to  other areas so with that I'd like to  thank you again for allowing me to  present at the meeting today and I'd be  happy to answer any questions thank you  thank you dr. Koh locks for that  informative presentation we will now  start the live Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click the send button well answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for let's get started  our first question asks what are your  plans for future improvements on sample  prep sequencing and data analysis yeah  thank you certainly so I focused on what  is possible today but we have obviously  plans to improve on all three aspects of  a sequencing project sample prep  sequencing and data analysis as you  mentioned and so just as a few examples  so in terms of sample preparation the  Express library kit that I mentioned  which is currently available for the  long insert cases we plan to make  available for all types of analysis so  amplicons for example 16s analysis and  so forth so that will be something that  is a future improvement on 